	<ArticleHeader title={"5 Lessons We Learned Scaling Our Startup to 10,000 Users"} excerpt={"The journey from 0 to 10,000 users taught us invaluable lessons about infrastructure, team dynamics, and customer focus."} author={"Alex Rivera"} date={"March 5, 2026"} image={{"url":"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1522071820081-009f0129c71c?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop","alt":"Team collaboration"}} />
	<BasicContent content={"<p><em>By Alex Rivera, Co-founder & CEO</em></p>\n\n<p>When we launched SaaSify two years ago, we had no idea what the journey from zero to 10,000 users would look like. Looking back, the path was filled with challenges we never anticipated and wins we couldn't have planned. Here are the five biggest lessons we learned along the way.</p>\n\n<h2>1. Your First Architecture Won't Scale</h2>\n<p>We started with a simple monolithic architecture. It was fast to build and easy to understand. By the time we hit 1,000 users, we were already running into performance issues. The database queries that worked fine with 100 users were bringing the system to its knees.</p>\n<p>The lesson? Build for today, but design with tomorrow in mind. We spent three months refactoring to a microservices architecture—time we could have saved with better initial planning.</p>\n\n<h2>2. Customer Support Is Product Development</h2>\n<p>In the early days, our entire team did customer support. It was exhausting, but it was also the best product research we could have done. Every support ticket revealed a gap in our UX, a missing feature, or a documentation failure.</p>\n<p>We still require every engineer to spend time on support each month. The insights are invaluable.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Hire Slow, Fire Fast</h2>\n<p>We made several hiring mistakes in our rush to grow. Each bad hire cost us months of productivity and damaged team morale. Now we take our time in the hiring process, involve multiple team members, and look for culture fit as much as technical skills.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Documentation Scales Better Than Meetings</h2>\n<p>When it was just the three founders, we could keep everything in our heads. By the time we hit 15 employees, tribal knowledge was killing us. We invested heavily in documentation—processes, decisions, architecture—and it paid dividends.</p>\n<p>New hires ramp up faster. Fewer interruptions. Better decisions.</p>\n\n<h2>5. Focus Is Everything</h2>\n<p>We said no to more features than we built. It was hard—every customer request felt urgent. But staying focused on our core use case allowed us to do one thing really well instead of many things poorly.</p>\n<p>The features we didn't build? Most of them would have been used by less than 5% of our users.</p>\n\n<h2>What's Next</h2>\n<p>We're now on the journey to 100,000 users, and I'm sure we'll learn even more lessons. If you're early in your startup journey, I hope these insights help you avoid some of the mistakes we made.</p>\n"} />
